From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vipul Cariappa <vipulcariappa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 20:25:35 +0530
Subject: Don't end a stream capture the failure path never began

When Python initialization fails, `%%python` ended two stdio captures
before either had been started -- the `BeginStdStreamCapture` calls sit
further down, on the success path. Popping an empty capture stack
segfaulted, so a legible "Failed to Initialize Python" turned into a dead
kernel and no diagnostic at all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit de3b938bdaa3798180693d1a4ea83c1671c2e73f)
---
 src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp | 6 ++----
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp b/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
index 5a7bfc0..22706aa 100644
--- a/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
+++ b/src/xmagics/pythonexec.cpp
@@ -22,10 +22,8 @@ namespace xcpp {
         if (!is_initalized)
             initialize();
         if (!is_initalized) {
-            // initializing failed    
-            std::cout << Cpp::EndStdStreamCapture();
-            std::cerr << Cpp::EndStdStreamCapture();
-
+            // Nothing has been captured yet -- the Begin calls below are what
+            // opens a capture -- so ending one here would pop an empty stack.
             std::cerr << "Failed to Initialize Python\n";
             return;
         }
